From: Junfeng Yang <didiglobalusa@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 01:44:33 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: flowtable: clear skb->offload_fwd_mark when
 forwarding

DSA taggers set skb->offload_fwd_mark on frames received on a port that
offloads a bridge, so that the software bridge does not forward them a
second time. When such a frame leaves the bridge towards the IP stack,
br_pass_frame_up() clears the mark again.

The flowtable ingress hook runs before the bridge rx handler. If the
flowtable is bound to the bridge port itself, a frame taken by the fast
path is routed and transmitted without ever passing br_pass_frame_up(),
and reaches the egress device with the mark still set.

On transmit the mark has a different meaning: it asks the driver to let
the hardware forward the frame on behalf of a bridge. tag_dsa acts on it
and builds a FORWARD tag. If the egress device is a standalone port of
the same switch, the frame goes out on the conduit with a FORWARD tag
that addresses no bridge, and it never arrives at the next hop.

This is seen on a Marvell 88E6176 (mv88e6xxx, EDSA tagging) with lan1 in
a bridge and wan standalone, and a flowtable bound to both ports. The
TCP handshake completes through the slow path; once the flow is added to
the flowtable every lan1->wan packet is sent with a FORWARD tag instead
of FROM_CPU and the connection stalls.

Clear the mark in the IPv4 and IPv6 forward paths, as the bridge does.

Signed-off-by: Junfeng Yang <didiglobalusa@gmail.com>
---

--- a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_ip.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_ip.c
@@ -376,6 +376,19 @@ nf_flow_offload_lookup(struct nf_flowtab
 	return flow_offload_lookup(flow_table, &tuple);
 }
 
+/* The flowtable ingress hook runs before the bridge rx handler, so a frame
+ * received on a switchdev bridge port never reaches br_pass_frame_up(), which
+ * is where the bridge clears skb->offload_fwd_mark before handing a frame to
+ * the IP stack. Clear it here as well: the frame is being routed, and must
+ * not be mistaken for one that the egress driver may forward in hardware.
+ */
+static void nf_flow_switchdev_frame_unmark(struct sk_buff *skb)
+{
+#ifdef CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV
+	skb->offload_fwd_mark = 0;
+#endif
+}
+
 static int nf_flow_offload_forward(struct nf_flowtable_ctx *ctx,
 				   struct nf_flowtable *flow_table,
 				   struct flow_offload_tuple_rhash *tuplehash,
@@ -416,6 +429,7 @@ static int nf_flow_offload_forward(struc
 
 	ip_decrease_ttl(iph);
 	skb_clear_tstamp(skb);
+	nf_flow_switchdev_frame_unmark(skb);
 
 	/* As ip_forward() does for the packets the flow did not take. */
 	if (flow->priority)
@@ -710,6 +724,7 @@ static int nf_flow_offload_ipv6_forward(
 
 	ip6h->hop_limit--;
 	skb_clear_tstamp(skb);
+	nf_flow_switchdev_frame_unmark(skb);
 
 	if (flow->priority)
 		skb->priority = flow->priority;
